                    "a": "Reference: The second stanza of 'If' by Rudyard Kipling. Explanation: The poet says: if you can dream without letting dreams master you, and think without making thoughts your aim; if you can meet Triumph and Disaster and treat those two impostors just the same; if you can bear to hear the truth you have spoken twisted by knaves to make a trap for fools, or watch the things you gave your life to broken, and stoop and build them up with worn-out tools — then you have courage, balance and the power to begin again.",
                    "q": "Explain lines 9–16: 'If you can dream ..... with worn-out tools.'"
