                    "a": "(d) More rapidly in myelinated than in non-myelinated axons — Myelinated axons conduct impulses more rapidly than unmyelinated axons because in myelinated axons the action potential is produced only at the nodes of Ranvier and jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction).",
                    "q": "(iv) Action potentials move along axons\n(a) More slowly in axons of large than in small diameter.\n(b) The direct action of acetylcholine on the axon membrane.\n(c) Activating the sodium-potassium pump at each point along the axon membrane.\n(d) More rapidly in myelinated than in non-myelinated axons."
