                    "a": "Scientists agree that our planet earth was once covered with water (marine) and the life forms originated in water, especially in hot springs called hydrothermal vents. The vents supplied the energy and raw material for the origin and survival of early forms of life. Archaebacteria can tolerate the extreme temperature of the hydrothermal vents; they had a catabolic metabolism to obtain energy from the complex compounds present around them. When these complex energy-rich compounds were gradually depleted, the evolving bacteria developed anabolic mechanisms of their own, and some heterotrophic bacteria transformed into autotrophic ones — first sulphur-bacteria and iron-bacteria, depending on simple inorganic substances, and later photosynthetic bacteria, which led to the gradual accumulation of oxygen in the environment.",
